  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        177.148447834047,
        -229.2061268518841
      ],
      "size": [
        792.671875,
        309
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 4,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Optical-flow frame interpolation over a REAL video, using a FOREACH loop.\n\nLoad a clip (the 'Load Video' node lists files in ComfyUI/input - copy images/pedestrian_area.mp4 there, or upload your own). 'Video Slice' keeps a short window so the frame batch stays small; 'Get Video Components' splits it into frames.\n\nA 'Data List Range' emits the pair indices 0..10. For each i the loop pulls consecutive frames (i, i+1) from the batch via 'Image From Batch' and interpolates their MIDDLE frame from the two endpoints ONLY (the true middle is never fed in):\n  1. Dense optical flow A->B and B->A (Farneback).\n  2. Warp A forward by t and B backward by 1-t along their flows ('Flow Map' -> cv2.remap); remap samples backward, so the scales are -t and t-1.\n  3. Blend the motion-compensated warps with weights (1-t) and t, then stamp 'interpolated' ('Draw Text').\n\nEvery pair is processed inside the loop. Because a 'Batch Images' accumulator cannot start empty, a BLACK PLACEHOLDER (ConstantLike -> CVToImage) seeds it; each iteration appends the ORIGINAL frame i then the interpolated middle, so the batch interleaves f0, mid(0,1), f1, mid(1,2), ... . After the loop 'Image From Batch' (index 1) strips the placeholder, leaving 22 frames (11 originals + 11 labeled middles). One knob: the 't' PrimitiveFloat (0.5 = middle) drives both warp scales and both blend weights.\n"
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
